//! run
//! stdout:
//| 4294967296 -4294967296
//| -2147483648 4611686018427387904
//| -2147483648 2147483648
//| 0 -4611686020574871552
//| 4294967297 4294967297
//| -2147483648 4611686018427387904
// The integer clause of D4.5 through a conditional: with no context an untyped
// integer becomes i32 if it fits and otherwise i64. A conditional whose
// condition is a run-time value folds to no value, so the constants in its arms
// reach the rule with nothing else to say what they are, and one type covers
// the whole expression (D6.2).
//
// This test stands beside run/constants/012, which reads the same rule from a
// shift with a variable count. Line 1 was refused with "constant 4294967296
// does not fit i32" until 2026-09-14.
//
// The type is read off the width, as run/constants/012 reads it: `<<` wraps at
// the width of its left operand (D6.2), so a count of 31 separates i32 from
// i64 in the value that is printed, and no program here aborts.
//
// Line 1: the value itself, in either arm, where i32 would be an error.
// Line 2: the boundary in the left arm. i32's maximum keeps i32 and one past it
// takes i64.
// Line 3: the boundary in the right arm, which the condition never chooses. The
// type belongs to the whole expression, so the arm that is not evaluated still
// decides it: the same `1` prints two values.
// Line 4: the negative end of the boundary. i32's minimum keeps i32, and one
// past it takes i64.
// Line 5: a constant outside the conditional joins the same expression.
// Line 6: an arm that folds. A node that folded stands for its whole subtree
// (D4.4), so `2147483648 - 1` is the i32 constant 2147483647 and
// `2147483647 + 1` is the i64 constant 2147483648.
fn z(i32 v) i32 {
    return v;
}

fn main() i32 {
    i32 n = z(1);
    i32 thirtyone = z(31);
    println(n > 0 ? 4294967296 : 1, " ", n > 0 ? -4294967296 : 1);
    println((n > 0 ? 2147483647 : 1) << thirtyone, " ", (n > 0 ? 2147483648 : 1) << thirtyone);
    println((n > 0 ? 1 : 2147483647) << thirtyone, " ", (n > 0 ? 1 : 2147483648) << thirtyone);
    println((n > 0 ? -2147483648 : 1) << thirtyone, " ", (n > 0 ? -2147483649 : 1) << thirtyone);
    println(1 + (n > 0 ? 4294967296 : 1), " ", (n > 0 ? 4294967296 : 1) + 1);
    println((n > 0 ? 2147483648 - 1 : 1) << thirtyone,
            " ",
            (n > 0 ? 2147483647 + 1 : 1) << thirtyone);
    return 0;
}
